I'm Nick Ryan I'm a music composer sound  designer artist and audio media  specialist my clients and my directors  often expect me to be conventional but  what they really want is a different  idea to everyone else I have to listen  really really carefully at the time most  people don't know how to listen and if  you learn how to listen  you'll be different to most people you  have to be able to care deeply about  what you do but not in isolation you  have to be able to collaborate with  other people and if you're collaborating  ultimately you have to be able to let go  of your work as part of the bigger  project  the first and most important thing was  to come up with a story and our previous  game Papa sangre who set in in Mexico in  a Mexican underworld and we decided to  set this one in space so we came up with  a story we came up with the location  then we started to edit levels and  create the actual structure of this this  this world and then we started to create  its inhabitants and be their monsters or  protagonists and then we started to  populate those levels and decide where  those different audio objects had to go  and then of course we had to play test  extensively one of the things we  encountered by in making an audio-only  game is that there's a huge amount of  subjective interpretation among our  players so it's quite important for us  to be sure that what we as game  designers think we're making is what our  players perceive it to be some of the  most significant decisions we made our  night jar were to firstly to build our  own game engine because nothing really  existed before this game to render 3d  sound in real time on a on a handheld  device and we could have we could have  used an existing engine but wouldn't  have really given us the flexibility  that we needed and in the long run would  have caused all sorts of other problems  such as licensing etcetera etcetera so  so we actually built our engine  ourselves so also when we decided to  make a game that was sound only we  realized we had to immerse the player in  a sound world that was believable and as  you know our own world is 3d its kind of  unremarkable we're used to listening in  3d but recordings are generally 2d  because they recorded with stereo  microphones and they don't really mimic  the way that we hear as humans so what  we used is a technique called binaural  recording and our game engine actually  renders sound in real time binaural E  and what that means is that we're  actually mimicking the way we hear as  humans  the reason we can tell with their sound  is not just to the left or the right of  us but also above all beneath us in  front of us or behind us is because  we've got two ears and our brains  actually make comparisons between what  our left ear hears my right ear hears  and it makes comparisons according to  volume time arrival and frequency and  when you put those three things together  you can actually create a 3d recording  and the easiest way to do that is to  place a microphone in each year and use  your head as the encoding tool if you  like the tools I use are some of some of  the tools I use a what everyone else  uses who's making sound logic audio and  Pro Tools but I also use lots of  specialist plugins lots especially  software to render sound in 3d I've got  one tool that's very good for placing  things behind you I've got one tool  that's very good for high-frequency  things and that's largely to do with the  shape of head that was used to make  their original algorithms I then use  various microphones including a dummy  head which has microphones in each year  and it looks like a crash crash test  dummy and we use that to actually make  make real recordings and in terms of the  game itself the game consists of  atmospheres which are loops objects  which move relative to you objects which  don't move relative to you which are  attached to your person like your  footsteps and also dialogue and dialogue  is is a vital component of this and we  were lucky enough to have Benedict  Cumberbatch as our main talent on this  the night John left you here as bait now  if the groove of the resolution are dead  and you will be - in minutes if you  don't get off this ship we had to make a  decision about whether to go for realism  over abstraction and what we discovered  was that we couldn't really go abstract  without exposition and it was a  fascinating process but  is what we ended up doing was trying to  use narrative narration became vital in  enabling our players to understand what  it was that they were listening to so  without any narration or any any sort of  narrative setup the interpretation our  players were open to was massive they  would perceive a completely different  space to each other so we had to set the  scene with fairly realistic sound sounds  that we have an innate recognition of  and an understanding of their physical  form we can actually visualize in our  imagination what object is making that  sound well once we've established that  we can then introduce providing there is  some explanation of it through narrative  we can actually introduce objects which  are more abstract and monsters for  example something in which we are we  expect to be abstract and we welcome  subjective interpretation of because  they then become much more frightening  the game did turn out mostly as we  expected it to but there are so-called  bugs which came from people's different  interpretations of what they were  hearing so we might have put in what we  thought was a monster and and and a  particular payer might have perceived it  to be a toilet flushing in the next-door  room rather than a monster crawling at  the wall you you know you never really  know so we had to extensively play test  this and then thun thun the actual  sounds as well in the absence of images  we can't help but hallucinate images in  our imagination as we do when we listen  to radio and that kind of age-old cliche  pitch is a better on radio stands with  the nightjar we you know we've said  jokingly that the night jar has the best  graphics engine in the world because  it's your imagination and what's  particularly exciting about being  nominated for the night jar for audio  achievement is that this is an audio  only  perience and that's that's quite a  unique proposition the thing that I'm  most proud of is that people who are  used to listening people who are blind  or visually impaired wrote to us and  told us that this was an incredibly  believable three-dimensional immersive  space and I would have expected they in  particular to have picked up on some of  the some of the the flaws in the sound  design and they they were very they were  very complimentary about it although  they did say that the games was far too  easy so we did have to make it harder  I'm also very proud of the fact that we  managed to keep our golden rule intact  the golden rule of all which was not to  have any visual representation of space  whatsoever and we were tempted  throughout the process to have some kind  of map but we we decided to be strict  with ourselves and now there is no  nothing to see you place your thumbs on  the screen you walk with your thumbs as  though they're your feet the interface  is intuitive and you can play the game  with your eyes closed
 

